=== layout: one-panel

# Topic 7: Nuclear remediation

@body

- Can a microbe make radioactive waste stop being radioactive?
- Microbes can change chemical form, bind radionuclides, or help immobilize them. They do not stop radioactive decay.
- At uranium sites, reducing soluble *U(VI)* to less-soluble *U(IV)* can limit movement in groundwater.
- The uranium is still there. What happens if oxygen returns?

@notes

2026 topic 7; numbering authority: ../old/2026_topic_list.txt.
Prompt source: ../old/Fall_2026_Talking_Points_Set_4-Environmental_and_Synthetic.pptx, slide 26.
Expanded instructor explanation and discussion, retaining the source questions. Historical student contributions are teaching leads, not scientific authority.
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3165427/

=== layout: two-panels

# 7. Surviving radiation vs. cleaning it up

@left

## Oak Ridge and Hanford

- Follow uranium and other radionuclides.
- Compare redox changes, sorption, and precipitation.
- Which results are field measurements?

@right

## Chernobyl and Fukushima

- What helps melanin-containing fungi or extremophiles survive?
- Survival alone does not prove cleanup.
- How could organisms capture cesium or strontium?

@notes

2026 topic 7; numbering authority: ../old/2026_topic_list.txt.
Prompt source: ../old/Fall_2026_Talking_Points_Set_4-Environmental_and_Synthetic.pptx, slide 26.
