=== layout: one-panel

# Topic 8: Heavy metal remediation

@body

- Microbes cannot digest a metal atom into something that is no longer a metal.
- They can change oxidation state, bind metals, take them up, or precipitate them.
- Chromium illustrates the point: reduction of *Cr(VI)* to *Cr(III)* often lowers mobility and toxicity under suitable conditions.
- Always ask where the metal went and whether it can move again.

@notes

2026 topic 8; numbering authority: ../old/2026_topic_list.txt.
Prompt source: ../old/Fall_2026_Talking_Points_Set_4-Environmental_and_Synthetic.pptx, slide 28.
Expanded instructor explanation and discussion, retaining the source questions. Historical student contributions are teaching leads, not scientific authority.
https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/pp1885I

=== layout: four-panels

# 8. Four ways to deal with a metal

@top-left

## Biosorption

Binding to a surface. Living growth is not always required.

@top-right

## Bioaccumulation

Uptake into cells. The contaminated biomass still needs handling.

@bottom-left

## Redox chemistry

Change oxidation state. The effect depends on the metal and conditions.

@bottom-right

## Precipitation

Form a less-soluble solid. Test whether changing conditions release it.

@notes

2026 topic 8; numbering authority: ../old/2026_topic_list.txt.
Prompt source: ../old/Fall_2026_Talking_Points_Set_4-Environmental_and_Synthetic.pptx, slide 28.
Expanded instructor explanation and discussion, retaining the source questions. Historical student contributions are teaching leads, not scientific authority.
https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/pp1885I

=== layout: one-panel

# 8. Metal resistance is not metal removal

@body

- *Berkeley Pit* and *Clark Fork*: how do acidity, mine drainage, and sediment chemistry affect treatment?
- *Hinkley*: which reactions and microbes reduce Cr(VI)? What happens to the resulting Cr(III)?
- Cupriavidus metallidurans (formerly Ralstonia): how do efflux pumps and metal-binding proteins protect cells?
- Mercury needs care: MerA can produce volatile Hg(0). Moving mercury into air is not destroying it. Compare binding, precipitation, and methylation risks.

@notes

2026 topic 8; numbering authority: ../old/2026_topic_list.txt.
Prompt source: ../old/Fall_2026_Talking_Points_Set_4-Environmental_and_Synthetic.pptx, slide 28.
Expanded instructor explanation and discussion, retaining the source questions. Historical student contributions are teaching leads, not scientific authority.
https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/pp1885I

=== layout: one-panel

# 8. How much metal can cells handle?

@body

- Would you use living cells, dead biomass, or an isolated binding protein? Why?
- How would you distinguish surface binding from uptake?
- What happens when the binding sites fill up, the pH changes, or several metals compete?
- At very high metal concentrations or in mixed waste, can the treatment organism still grow?

@notes

2026 topic 8; numbering authority: ../old/2026_topic_list.txt.
Prompt source: ../old/Fall_2026_Talking_Points_Set_4-Environmental_and_Synthetic.pptx, slide 28.
Expanded instructor explanation and discussion, retaining the source questions. Historical student contributions are teaching leads, not scientific authority.
https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/pp1885I

=== layout: one-panel

# Topic 9: Forever chemical remediation

@body

- PFAS is a large family of chemicals, not one molecule with one cleanup method.
- Many carbon-fluorine bonds resist biological breakdown. The exact PFAS structure matters.
- A lab organism that transforms one PFAS has not solved every contaminated aquifer.
- [*Defluorination*]{underline=true} means breaking carbon-fluorine bonds. Where is the fluoride, and what organic products remain?

@notes

2026 topic 9; numbering authority: ../old/2026_topic_list.txt.
Prompt source: ../old/Fall_2026_Talking_Points_Set_4-Environmental_and_Synthetic.pptx, slide 30.
Expanded instructor explanation and discussion, retaining the source questions. Historical student contributions are teaching leads, not scientific authority.
https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39018407/

=== layout: one-panel

# 9. Removed from water... then what?

@body

| Process | Where did the PFAS go? | What should we measure? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sorption | Onto carbon, resin, or biomass | Spent material and breakthrough |
| Membrane separation | Into a concentrated waste stream | Concentrate and treated water |
| Biotransformation | Into other molecules | Products and fluoride release |
| Destruction claim | Bonds broken toward final products | A convincing fluorine balance |

