=== layout: one-panel

# Topic 11: Greenhouse gas remediation

@body

- Microbes make greenhouse gases. Other microbes consume them. Can we change the balance?
- [*Methanotrophs*]{underline=true} use methane; methane monooxygenase starts oxidation to methanol.
- Carbon can become biomass or continue toward carbon dioxide. Follow both routes.
- Converting methane to carbon dioxide can reduce warming impact, but it is not the same as permanently removing carbon.

@notes

2026 topic 11; numbering authority: ../old/2026_topic_list.txt.
Prompt source: ../old/Fall_2026_Talking_Points_Set_4-Environmental_and_Synthetic.pptx, slide 34.
Expanded instructor explanation and discussion, retaining the source questions. Historical student contributions are teaching leads, not scientific authority.
https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsbiofuels

=== layout: four-panels

# 11. Where does the carbon go?

@top-left

## Methane oxidation

CH4 to methanol and downstream products. Compare pMMO and sMMO in methanotrophs.

@top-right

## Carbon fixation

CO2 into organic molecules. Compare the Calvin cycle with reverse TCA and other pathways.

@bottom-left

## Storage

Carbon in soils, biomass, or minerals. How long does it stay there?

@bottom-right

## Fuel or chemical

Capture carbon into a product. Burning that fuel releases the carbon again.

@notes

