# Topic 13: Rare earth mining

@body

- Can we persuade biology to separate metals that chemists struggle to separate?
- [*Bioleaching*]{underline=true} helps release metals from a solid. *Biosorption* captures dissolved ions on a surface.
- Binding a lot of metal and selectively binding the metal you want are different achievements.
- Lanmodulin proteins provide a useful example of selective rare-earth binding.

@notes

2026 topic 13; numbering authority: ../old/2026_topic_list.txt.
Prompt source: ../old/Fall_2026_Talking_Points_Set_4-Environmental_and_Synthetic.pptx, slide 38.
Expanded instructor explanation and discussion, retaining the source questions. Historical student contributions are teaching leads, not scientific authority.
https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-05945-5
https://www.nature.com/articles/s41589-026-02176-3

=== layout: two-panels

# 13. Get it out. Then separate it.

@left

## Release the metal

- Microbial acids and ligands can help mobilize it.
- The ore or waste chemistry matters.
- What acid, water, and energy inputs remain?

@right

## Recover the metal

- Bind ions to biomass or a protein.
- Separate competing metals.
- Release the product and reuse the binding material.

@notes

